continuing our look at the very  competitive three races for Clark County  Commission today we're focusing on  District G Billy Mitchell running as a  republican for that seat Jim Gibson's  seat now the incumbent uh Mr Mitchell  good to meet you sir good to meet you  too uh so let's talk about it you were  talking before we started that you've  never run for political office before uh  what motivated you to run and for the  viewers at home for the voters at home  uh some of your background  so um I'm a veteran I was the ex-school  teacher I raced NASCAR I built the  fountains Bellagio a lot of different  engineering projects I'm an engineering  project manager  um I just got to a point where I just  couldn't sit back anymore you know I  defended two Wars and this isn't the  country I remember growing up so I felt  like I needed to get involved and at the  county level seems to be the appropriate  place so what is it at the county level  that you would want to address first  we need to do it all  we need to go through and be more  accountable for the money that's being  spent and what projects I'm working for  DOD now I'm very familiar with  government processes and from what I've  seen at the County Commission meetings  they're not being followed  explain a little bit about that what  what do you mean specifically that's not  being followed give me an example of  something that stands out to you that  you would like to deal with right away  so the  firm to me was I was at the water  commissioning meeting a couple weeks ago  and they just gave him 7.1 million  dollars right off the cuff  the way I'm used to dealing with the  Department of Defense is you have to put  out rfps you have to do a bidding  process and you have to justify that  financing before you can just say  spend 7.1 million dollars on you know  digitizing records or whatever the  excuse here we're giving them so you  you're uh you think that sort of there's  a blank check at the County Commission  that you want to try and deal with oh  definitely I've seen it uh the three  meetings I've been at even with the  voting machines uh Gloria stood up there  and asked for you know another three  hundred thousand dollars and said he  needed it today in order to upgrade  their machines and they did it they it  turned right to the clerk and said he'd  get it to him today you don't you don't  believe they're uh their their issues  and their what they're bringing to the  County Commission is in good faith well  I don't think anybody even looks at it I  think they just could bring it up but  you got seven Democrats that are on  there if you look at some of their  policies during the covid mandates and  everything I don't agree with it I don't  think they were in good faith I think  they are doing what they're told from  the governor and they just fall in line  with their party  we're going to touch on on covet again  in just a second here but let's talk  about some of the other issues specific  to District G although I guess this is  an issue for all of us it's it's the  issue of water  um what do you think needs to be done  from a Clark County commission level to  help with our water shortages our water  issues and what's happening out at Lake  Mead so I think the federal government  needs to get involved and I think that  our County Commissioners and our County  needs to facilitate that that pact that  they have or agreement was from 1920s  they got to a two-way level just a few  weeks ago when the County Commission was  up there the reality is that you know  based off the information that the  Southern Nevada Water Authority  presented to the county that we're not  using any more water now than we did 10  years ago so why is the lake going dry  well it's real simple California is  using way over their allotment when they  got to a 2A which is a tier one level  they were all supposed to come to the  together and come back with cutbacks we  did that a matter of fact I call them  the Gibson mandates now with the pools  and the front yards that they just put  in  um California didn't even come to the  table so at that point what the county  should have done and what the state  should have done is they should have  filed a federal lawsuit against  California for breach of contract and at  that point it would have forced them to  come to the table before it gets down to  a 2B level which the Southern Nevada  Water Authority said is going to be in  less than 24 months and they may have to  shut the dam down do you support some of  those measures acknowledging the issues  with the federal other states and that's  that's a whole other tier but just  specifically the Clark County you  mentioned the Gibson mandates  referencing Jim Gibson do you support  those measures that were implemented by  the county whether it's the the pools uh  the moving removing of Lawns uh do you  support those well I think the big  picture here and now the studies are  coming out that you know as we remove  the Lawns and put in the turf I know  from coach and football that Turf gets  very hot it gets to a point you know it  looks great on the surface but until you  got to play on it I mean the surface  temperature at the high schools with the  turf is well over you know 150 degrees  you got to play with you know long  sleeves so it's going to increase the  temperatures here in Clark County so  what effect will it have on the water  I don't think it's going to have but  maybe .01 of the water usage I think our  Monica and our time to enforce all that  can be used in other ways to like  Boulder City needs a new water treatment  plant which is in my district we could  be using that budget to do that and try  and reclaim water more effectively than  we're doing than to come out to the  constituencies and say okay well we're  going to start regulating you more with  bigger government  you mentioned this a few seconds ago  you're talking about the covid mandates  um how would you have handled  coronavirus whether it's from a a  mandate standpoint with masks or with an  economic aspect with funding and so  forth if you were in the Clark County  Commission two years ago in the depths  of the pandemic what would you have done  so I I looked at it you know serving in  the military and overcome this is  biological warfare okay and they used it  to instill fear this is right out of  Marxism and Machiavelli 101 template  okay you put fear in people fear the  unknown and you can actually get them to  do whatever you want if we compare our  state to other states that did not do  all the things that we did  they're doing better economically did it  affect or curve anything no the reports  came from CDC and from John Hopkins  University that the only thing they  actually did was killed 40 percent of  small businesses and they actually  killed our schools 20 percent of our  kids have not come back to school and 40  percent of small businesses which is the  middle class are gone they're gone they  have never reopened and that's another  issue that we need to facilitate to try  and get those small businesses back  there's 23 steps right now it's way too  expensive you look from anything from an  Uber driver to Mom and Pop shops it's  just accounting can be doing more to try  and facilitate that growth so what more  can be done moving forward if the past  can't be changed what would you do if  you're elected to sort of bring back  that help for small business so we need  to streamline that we need to streamline  the processes as far as getting your  small business the other thing is a  taxation we we're in a recession they  don't want to admit it they're  gaslighting everybody but we're in a  recession so we need to look at it from  a county level and take a look at what  the fees we're charging stop spending  all the money and say okay what can we  do to bring our economy back that's our  number one priority second priority is  obviously law enforcement okay violent  crime is going through the roof right  now I've every time I turn on your news  every morning they're talking about  somebody got stabbed or somebody got  killed I I don't even recognize Las  Vegas that I grew up here this is my  home  um what do you think District G needs  that the incumbent is not providing so  he  even represent District G I've had the  police department the fire departments  all come to me the people and and  Boulder City  um he's a career politician when he left  Henderson he left him with a 90 Min  million dollar deficit that left  everybody with the bad taste in their  mouth when he first got appointed  to county commissioner  he shouldn't have he wasn't elected he  shouldn't he lost the mayor race he  didn't even get through the primary with  Dina Titus he should have just said  that's the end of politics but he didn't  he decided to throw his hat in the ring  at County Commissioner and I don't think  he's really represented Henderson or or  um Boulder City well at all and how  would you as someone who's never been in  politics represented better so I think  we need to look at again small  businesses we need to support our law  enforcement our detention facilities we  need to look at it and say okay what do  these people need to do their job what  do these people need to actually get  their businesses back up and running  water Street District Boulder City  these these small mom and pop shops need  all the help they can get right now and  whether that's just easing the fees  reducing taxes or streamlining the  licensing process through the county it  needs to be done one of the things that  should have been is is not a county  issue it is a county issue but it's not  kind of commission issue is the school  district we've asked candidates high and  low both all political parties about  what can be done break it up keep it  together what changes need to be done  what do you think you would like what  would you like to see done on a County  commission level to try and improve  things with CCSD well the first step is  counting commission means to get  involved okay the County Commission has  no authority over the Clark County  School District which when I first found  that out kind of blew my mind because  they have authority over everything else  including Metro and everything else so  what do we do I'm I'm an ex-teacher I  taught and coached football Coronado  High School in Henderson I saw firsthand  what what the principals and the  teachers are going through they're not  getting the support they need and now  with this new culture that's going  around cancel culture whatever you want  to call it they're just not getting the  backup that they need to do their job  and then we need to look at the  standards because we really need to  change change the standards because the  federal money right now is dictating the  standards and it's it's not doing well  for our students where are our students  at four or five years from now they're  not graduating from college they don't  have a trade school which we need to  really facilitate I'm a big CTE fan  obviously so we need to maybe look at  these process see what we can do to  actually improve our community and  prepare our youth for the future  well good luck in the election District  G's looks like a very interesting race  obviously the incumbent Jim Gibson and  Billy Mitchell who's running right now  for Clark County Commission Billy thank  you thank you

